WEBVTT

03:58.444 --> 04:02.036
Maybe if I start talking, the conversation will slow down. So I keep telling these

04:02.068 --> 04:05.564
guys we're about ready. And, uh, so we're gonna go ahead and get

04:05.596 --> 04:07.617
started. I just kind of as a— oh,

04:07.745 --> 04:11.353
everybody's here. I thought you were online. I was gonna make sure. Is everybody

04:11.369 --> 04:14.800
online and working good? Okay, then we'll go ahead and

04:15.300 --> 04:18.656
call this meeting to order at 3:06. Thank you. It's our budget work session,

04:18.736 --> 04:22.716
and I'm pretty excited to see where this goes. So our first

04:22.845 --> 04:26.323
item was— are you first,

04:26.404 --> 04:30.549
or— all I have is discussion proposed budget. So I'm,

04:31.224 --> 04:35.015
I'm actually really proud to see you like full dress uniform. I don't

04:35.515 --> 04:36.306
know if I should salute,

04:39.106 --> 04:42.489
but I did invite— you can come on.

04:43.069 --> 04:46.261
You can come on up, but we— I invited Chief Smith. I know he said

04:46.761 --> 04:50.344
he probably had things to do, but he trusted you'd probably give a better presentation

04:50.442 --> 04:53.619
anyway, so. That's not true. I would say I can come out here.

04:57.021 --> 04:58.304
I'm going to give him a full report.

05:17.672 --> 05:28.020
Okay. Any technical—

05:28.520 --> 05:31.770
oh, oh, something's happening. There you go. Ta-da! Alrighty.

05:34.126 --> 05:37.829
Mayor, City Council, Mr. Reeves, I appreciate you guys giving me the

05:38.329 --> 05:41.933
time to meet with you today to talk about the budget request.

05:42.093 --> 05:45.315
And again, Sheriff Smith sends his regards. He's not going to be here.

05:45.363 --> 05:49.210
I talked to him earlier. He did give me a little comment to

05:49.710 --> 05:53.170
maybe present to you at the end. You know, he's obviously passionate about

05:53.218 --> 05:57.193
the contract, about being the sheriff of the county, and his

05:57.290 --> 06:00.864
expectations of our law enforcement service that we provide, not only to the residents

06:01.377 --> 06:04.856
of the county, but the residents of Eagle Mountain specifically. So I'll address

06:05.356 --> 06:08.998
that later. Thank you. Please forgive his absence, and appreciate

06:09.498 --> 06:11.696
you letting me take a little bit of time. What I want to talk about

06:12.196 --> 06:15.534
today—I kind of gave a similar presentation a little over a year ago,

06:16.160 --> 06:19.340
or around this time frame. For those of you that weren't here as council members

06:19.436 --> 06:22.777
then, this some of it will be new. Some of you that were here before,

06:22.905 --> 06:26.663
it'll be stuff you're familiar with to a certain extent. I'll just kind of cut

06:27.163 --> 06:30.486
to the chase and get to what I'm requesting, and and and it's about

06:30.534 --> 06:33.648
some staffing plan needs for the. for the Sheriff's Office here

06:34.148 --> 06:37.191
at Eagle Mountain. I leave this slide up. It's the beginning one. It's kind of

06:37.691 --> 06:41.136
what we do. I won't read it to you verbatim, but the top line

06:41.636 --> 06:44.872
is important, and I think it's very important. We are your police department, and we

06:45.372 --> 06:48.672
provide police services to the citizens of Eagle Mountain. We've done so

06:49.172 --> 06:52.456
since the inception of Eagle Mountain, and, and now it's,

06:52.568 --> 06:55.743
it's an official contract. We are not called out here

06:55.791 --> 06:59.864
daily for calls for service. We are assigned out here. We have an actual—

07:00.364 --> 07:04.463
What's your name? Office space that we utilize. We have 35

07:04.479 --> 07:07.323
sworn law enforcement officers and some administrative staff.

07:07.371 --> 07:10.392
So let me get to that right here. This is, this is who we are

07:10.521 --> 07:13.735
in a nutshell. Um, we are the Eagle Mountain Division

07:13.946 --> 07:17.454
of the Sheriff's Office. You have a chief deputy, that's me. Um,

07:17.567 --> 07:20.801
then you have a lieutenant, you have an officer specialist, and you have 5 sergeants.

07:21.431 --> 07:24.951
Uh, one is our admin sergeant and the other 4 are patrol sergeants, and they

07:24.983 --> 07:28.579
each have, uh, 4 deputies assigned to them. And then you

07:29.079 --> 07:32.393
have one victim advocate, two special victims, two property crime detectives for a

07:32.441 --> 07:35.706
total of four detectives and the victim advocate. Also,

07:36.987 --> 07:40.450
under Sergeant Max Morgan, you also have three coordinators who deal with things like our

07:40.950 --> 07:44.770
RAD program, and then you have the VIPs, the volunteers in patrol. So down

07:45.270 --> 07:47.285
at the bottom right, it tells you kind of what our exact staffing is.

07:49.460 --> 07:53.628
Tell you kind of the layout. Our current service demand and coverage is 77,000

07:54.128 --> 07:57.679
people is approximately maybe just a little hair less than that 76. Uh, number.

07:57.743 --> 08:01.558
Maybe even we're over 77 now. Um, 50.3 square miles.

08:02.055 --> 08:05.742
We do run 24/7 patrol operations. We have 4 patrol

08:05.822 --> 08:09.316
teams, as I mentioned, 1 sergeant, 4 deputies per shift. We run 12-hour shifts,

08:10.150 --> 08:13.949
um, basically a day and a night. They rotate about every 2 months.

08:14.253 --> 08:16.914
The, the teams will go to day shift for 2 months and then back to

08:16.962 --> 08:20.537
graveyards for 2 months. We have 2 animal control deputies.

08:20.585 --> 08:23.759
We have 2 traffic enforcement for a total of 24 patrol officers.

08:24.631 --> 08:28.073
on duty or as part of our contract.

08:29.827 --> 08:31.516
Now, at the bottom of that, you see some numbers.

08:32.977 --> 08:36.291
What happened last year after this presentation was given was the request,

08:36.307 --> 08:41.303
and it was the correct request, to give you a— not

08:41.319 --> 08:45.021
just a number of officers per 1,000 as why we need to expand,

08:45.102 --> 08:48.512
but what's our workload? What's the workload of the deputies that are assigned to work

08:49.012 --> 08:51.503
out here? And so, I did do a workload study, or at least I went

08:52.003 --> 08:53.682
through one and tried to do it the best I could and get as accurate

08:54.182 --> 08:57.580
as I can. I wanted you to see these numbers because I want you to

08:58.080 --> 09:01.280
kind of understand just a simple number, whether we need more or

09:01.780 --> 09:05.280
not. This number reflects how many people are assigned out here. At the current

09:05.780 --> 09:08.880
number of sworn officers, we have 0.45 officers per 1,000,

09:09.380 --> 09:13.130
or 1 per 2,200 residents. Lehigh City has 0.7,

09:13.160 --> 09:16.740
or 1 per 1,429. They're at about 100,000,

09:16.980 --> 09:20.380
maybe a little bit more than 100,000 people. Saratoga Springs and

09:20.880 --> 09:23.660
Bluffdale— I added Bluffdale because I was asked the question a while ago. I was

09:24.160 --> 09:26.930
what going to ask you that. those actual numbers are, but they are at about

09:27.430 --> 09:30.770
1 per 1,000. If you divide them up, I think there's 14 patrol officers

09:31.270 --> 09:34.970
that are assigned to Bluffdale, and then there's another 60-something officers that are assigned

09:35.470 --> 09:38.690
to full-time Saratoga Springs. So they're at—

09:39.190 --> 09:42.490
Saratoga Springs itself is about 0.9 per 1,000, Bluffdale is about 0.7

09:42.990 --> 09:46.450
per 1,000, but combined they're about 1 officer per 1,000 for

09:46.950 --> 09:50.131
the total population of both cities. Mm-hmm. They're close by.

09:50.487 --> 09:53.139
That's why I'm including them. I want you to kind of see what the surrounding

09:53.511 --> 09:57.183
cities have as part of that workload study.

09:58.960 --> 10:02.275
Lehigh City did one at the request of their city council

10:02.291 --> 10:06.335
members. They referred to the ICMA, which is the International City Council

10:06.835 --> 10:10.278
Management Association. I refer to the same thing. We have something

10:10.294 --> 10:14.187
similar in law enforcement, the International Association of Chiefs of Police, that also gives some

10:14.687 --> 10:17.396
standards. when it comes to law enforcement services in a city.

10:17.492 --> 10:20.922
Mm-hmm. Some of the things that the ICMA talks about

10:21.002 --> 10:24.657
in their initial study is what's called the Rule of 60.

10:25.090 --> 10:28.264
The Rule of 60 is your patrol allocation. The Rule of 60 in this

10:28.376 --> 10:32.319
case says 60 of the sworn should be assigned— 60% of the sworn should

10:32.819 --> 10:36.519
be assigned to patrol. Currently, we have 68.5% assigned to patrol here in Eagle

10:36.551 --> 10:39.821
Mountain, which puts us above the benchmark. However,

10:39.837 --> 10:43.275
we are light in administration, supervision, investigations and

10:43.775 --> 10:47.408
community outreach programs with the city. But we are light on admin,

10:47.424 --> 10:49.952
but we are slightly heavy on regular patrol.

10:52.230 --> 10:56.158
The ICMA refers to the 75% rule or 75% availability,

10:56.190 --> 10:58.858
and what they're talking about, that basically is what it says in the parentheses,

10:58.955 --> 11:02.271
meaning roughly 25% of the officers are unavailable to give a shift due to

11:02.771 --> 11:06.688
court and leave and training. Again, we're talking about authorized versus what's really deployable.

11:07.306 --> 11:09.937
We have— we are authorized 24 guys on patrol,

11:10.432 --> 11:14.027
But what you actually patrol with is about the

11:14.527 --> 11:17.332
75% range because a lot of the guys are called to do different things,

11:17.348 --> 11:19.980
whether they're on sick leave, FMLA, whether they're in court,

11:20.221 --> 11:23.815
and things like that. So even though we're authorized

11:24.315 --> 11:27.362
at 24, effectively our deployment

11:27.862 --> 11:31.390
strength is consistently lowered due to leave, injuries, training, specialty commitments.

11:31.454 --> 11:34.487
That's not unique to Eagle Mountain. That is just law enforcement in general.

11:35.305 --> 11:39.108
And so we at Eagle Mountain, we try to maintain 4

11:39.157 --> 11:42.805
deputies on shift at a time. A lot of times as we move

11:43.305 --> 11:45.953
forward doing that, the calls for service is requiring us to use a little bit

11:46.453 --> 11:48.600
more overtime to call in deputies to work those shifts.

11:51.190 --> 11:54.445
So our workload study, we have 25,000,

11:54.462 --> 11:58.560
or last year we had 25,699 or 98

11:58.673 --> 12:02.470
total calls for service. That's an 18.6%

12:02.486 --> 12:05.115
increase of calls for service from the year prior.

12:05.635 --> 12:09.620
Eric? Yes, sir. Commensurate with the growth? Excuse me,

12:10.102 --> 12:13.795
that number, do you think like that, that's commensurate with the overall growth

12:14.295 --> 12:17.568
of the citizens, or do you feel like that that's an increase in just

12:18.068 --> 12:21.374
actual crime? It's an increase with the citizens. Um,

12:21.566 --> 12:24.713
I'll go out on a quick tangent real quick.

12:26.158 --> 12:29.177
Eagle Mountain City's safe, and, and, and I hope that's not being

12:29.273 --> 12:32.966
presented differently. Sometimes I'll read in social media, you know, we're turning into I

12:33.466 --> 12:35.617
hate to use a city like West Valley, but they say things like that.

12:36.580 --> 12:39.648
I think West Valley City is relatively safe too.

12:39.729 --> 12:43.358
They have their share of problems, but Eagle Mountain is not West Valley City.

12:44.531 --> 12:47.294
We don't have that crime rate. We are one of the safest cities in the

12:47.794 --> 12:51.084
state of Utah. You have citizen increase,

12:51.197 --> 12:54.457
which is creating more calls for service, more both law

12:54.473 --> 12:58.135
enforcement initiated and calls for service from the citizens

12:58.635 --> 13:01.187
itself, from 911 calls and other various ways that they call into the— Thank you.

13:01.397 --> 13:05.234
the Sheriff's Office to get assistance. So I would say that's

13:05.734 --> 13:09.833
attributed more to population growth. That comes

13:10.333 --> 13:13.815
out to about 70 incidents per day. That's 32 minutes average per incident,

13:14.525 --> 13:18.103
38 hours per day spent handling calls for service. And I tell you this

13:18.120 --> 13:21.758
simply because this plays into the workload study.

13:22.664 --> 13:26.235
This doesn't include the backup units. When you do this

13:26.268 --> 13:28.858
study, you're trying to get as much real data as possible.

13:29.456 --> 13:33.464
There's only so much our computer systems can provide that we can analyze. We have

13:33.964 --> 13:37.696
to guesstimate on the rest. I chose not to guesstimate too much information on

13:37.712 --> 13:41.399
there. I didn't want to throw in the number of backup officers coming to a

13:41.899 --> 13:43.899
call if I didn't have the right answer for you, so I simply plugged in

13:44.399 --> 13:47.731
the data that I had. It didn't include the backup units. It didn't include report

13:48.231 --> 13:52.203
time or travel time— travel time to a call, but also travel time checking

13:52.219 --> 13:55.425
on duty, coming to work, and checking off duty and leaving work.

13:56.655 --> 13:59.283
And so, the Sheriff's Office, we,

14:00.288 --> 14:01.002
the deputies,

14:03.240 --> 14:06.181
check on duty from their homes, get in their vehicles, and drive to work,

14:06.245 --> 14:09.855
and they are paid for that travel time coming into work. Just a

14:09.903 --> 14:13.405
follow-up question with the backup officers.

14:13.662 --> 14:17.438
Generally speaking, there's a protocol, I'm assuming, the number of backup

14:17.938 --> 14:21.128
officers that come depending on the type of call that it is?

14:21.483 --> 14:24.456
I wouldn't list it as a protocol, but generally, just for officer safety, we want

14:24.956 --> 14:27.278
to try to, depending on the Depending on the call, we want at least 2

14:27.778 --> 14:31.196
there. If you have a domestic violence, typically the deputy that shows up on scene,

14:31.212 --> 14:34.488
if he's not hearing an obvious reason he needs to go in by himself,

14:34.504 --> 14:37.908
he will wait for a backup officer. Very typical of a domestic violence

14:37.956 --> 14:40.509
call because we know through experience those are some of the most dangerous calls we

14:41.009 --> 14:43.143
respond to. I just want to make some assumptions here, and maybe this is helpful

14:43.175 --> 14:47.478
to you. So if you have 4 officers on duty and

14:48.008 --> 14:51.460
3 of them are on a single call, we now have 1, 1 person to

14:51.589 --> 14:54.600
answer the next call and then have to start Parsing out,

14:55.100 --> 14:58.360
correct? It's very common. And not only is it common just for the type of

14:58.860 --> 15:01.580
calls, but, but take a DUI for instance. That's just another example. If you have

15:02.080 --> 15:04.760
a DUI, you make an arrest, the only jail facility that's close to us is

15:05.260 --> 15:08.360
Spanish Fork. So now you're going to lose him for an hour to drive down

15:08.860 --> 15:11.860
there, half hour for booking, and an hour to come back. So you're going to

15:12.360 --> 15:13.950
lose people. You can make it 3 on a call and have 1 available.

15:14.040 --> 15:16.500
And there'll be a lot— there are a lot of times where you won't have

15:17.000 --> 15:20.100
anybody available. And, and we sometimes will call on either the North

15:20.600 --> 15:23.835
County unit or surrounding agency to come assist us on that call. Call.

15:24.138 --> 15:25.300
So that happens quite frequently.

15:28.414 --> 15:31.727
So the ICMA talks about the workload, and the number that they focus on is

15:31.775 --> 15:35.395
what's called the saturated index. In other words, your staffing

15:35.428 --> 15:38.721
needs. It's the total number of officers committed to calls per

15:38.737 --> 15:42.781
hour divided by the total officers' time available per hour. They give

15:43.281 --> 15:46.462
the standard here. This came through ICMA itself. It said if it

15:46.692 --> 15:50.372
is consistently under 50%, you're likely your staffing is

15:50.470 --> 15:54.742
cushioned. If you're at about 50 to 60%, you are properly

15:54.775 --> 15:57.899
staffed. If you run over 60% for sustained periods,

15:57.931 --> 16:01.515
you are understaffed. In our case, running the numbers,

16:01.612 --> 16:05.211
our patrol saturation level is about 65 to 70% average

16:05.244 --> 16:08.747
during most periods and higher during peak hours. Peak hours

16:08.859 --> 16:11.831
vary, but typically peak hours on afternoon and in the weekends.

16:12.072 --> 16:15.607
Typically you run into about the 3 in the afternoon

16:16.107 --> 16:19.613
till about 7 or 8 o'clock at night. Usually you're dealing with traffic at that

16:20.113 --> 16:22.227
time when people are coming and going. You also have peak times in the morning

16:22.292 --> 16:25.464
as well. And so, but typically we're at about

16:25.496 --> 16:28.972
65 to 70% work saturation or saturation

16:29.472 --> 16:33.187
index. That's what the level is. Our deputies are spending most of their available

16:33.203 --> 16:36.486
time responding to calls, which limits their ability to do proactive work.

16:36.824 --> 16:39.496
And I'll get into more later about what I'm— when I talk about proactive work.

16:40.477 --> 16:43.202
But so the question becomes, how many do we need? Yeah.

16:44.639 --> 16:48.957
It's a tricky question, and, and obviously I think this is why I

16:48.973 --> 16:52.680
was asked to look at a workload, take the workload

16:53.180 --> 16:55.313
stance to find out what kind of work they're doing and not just base it

16:55.813 --> 16:59.823
off officers per 1,000 residents. And I think that's wise. I think

17:00.323 --> 17:03.851
that International Chiefs of Police Association is, is suggesting the

17:04.351 --> 17:08.811
same thing. But based on our call volume, our staffing level per shift

17:09.211 --> 17:12.521
Eagle Mountain needs about 5 to 6 deputies per shift as a minimum. We run

17:13.021 --> 17:16.376
about 4. Assigned to patrol, we should have

17:16.876 --> 17:20.518
about 32 to 34 deputies assigned to patrol. That keeps us at that

17:20.777 --> 17:24.209
cushioned level. An overall increase of about

17:24.274 --> 17:27.350
8 to 10 deputies for patrol is, is what we need to

17:27.399 --> 17:30.846
have. Industry best practices focus on maintaining

17:31.346 --> 17:35.069
enough deputies on duty to handle calls while still allowing proactive police

17:35.280 --> 17:37.943
and safe backup. Proactive police,

17:39.849 --> 17:43.404
we're talking about community outreach programs. We're talking about the

17:43.904 --> 17:47.040
ability to work on neighborhood issues on a more long-term basis.

17:47.832 --> 17:51.873
We work shift work. A deputy working the day, working with a citizen

17:52.373 --> 17:54.880
on a particular project, might spend a day with them and then transfer to day

17:55.380 --> 17:58.413
shift, and now this person has to deal with another officer. So what we're trying

17:58.913 --> 18:01.653
to do as part of my request is going to be for some— a couple

18:02.153 --> 18:05.554
of full-time community-oriented police officers that can be

18:05.570 --> 18:09.263
out there working on long-term community issues. That's part

18:09.763 --> 18:12.860
of the patrol staffing plan, to increase patrol, but increase by 2

18:12.876 --> 18:16.328
guys that I can use for strict proactive work to help with quality of life

18:16.828 --> 18:20.198
in neighborhoods. We're having that now. You have a lot of complaints that come forward.

18:20.246 --> 18:22.927
One of the conversations I had with the sheriff had to do with— he was

18:22.975 --> 18:26.765
here last night on a meeting— and it's

18:27.265 --> 18:30.936
visibility of law enforcement in the community. Eagle Mountain's

18:31.436 --> 18:35.104
lacking there. We're lacking on having our deputies getting out to areas because

18:35.120 --> 18:38.534
now they're becoming more inundated with calls for service. Right. Now, when we talk

18:39.034 --> 18:41.548
about calls for service, even keep in mind, there's still part of that calls for

18:42.048 --> 18:45.203
service number is some proactive work. Now, we're talking traffic stops.

18:46.165 --> 18:49.852
We increased over the last couple of years our traffic

18:50.352 --> 18:53.891
unit to 2 full-time traffic, traffic units that help with calls as well

18:54.004 --> 18:58.048
when they can. But they are doing traffic enforcement.

18:58.498 --> 19:01.356
And as you probably saw, some of you that see the numbers, that number has

19:01.484 --> 19:04.904
increased on traffic enforcement. They are out being very proactive,

19:05.418 --> 19:08.870
and they're writing a lot of tickets. But they're being pulled off also

19:09.370 --> 19:12.419
to handle calls when patrol gets— when regular deputies

19:12.919 --> 19:16.642
are tied up. So that's what the need is. Chief? Go ahead.

19:16.883 --> 19:20.174
You mentioned the community policing and being proactive and different things like

19:20.674 --> 19:23.954
that. And we hear up here on the dais quite a bit from residents when

19:25.183 --> 19:28.739
people are doing different things, and we hear little pockets of it throughout

19:29.239 --> 19:32.547
the city. Are— and you're here when you hear— when we hear those things.

19:32.984 --> 19:36.784
Right. Are there any of those common concerns that are expressed by residents

19:37.188 --> 19:41.735
that are very unique

19:42.287 --> 19:45.438
and would be great for community policing to help tackle some of them?

19:46.764 --> 19:50.023
So like e-bikes, some of those other things that we're hearing from people?

19:50.521 --> 19:53.892
So great question. One of the discussions that have been made

19:53.908 --> 19:57.729
or being— that are being had is, is how do you enforce city

19:57.826 --> 20:02.193
ordinance and how does Utah County Sheriff's Office enforce

20:02.693 --> 20:07.186
city ordinance? And I know the discussion came up about getting

20:07.699 --> 20:11.312
our city ordinances onto the computer

20:11.812 --> 20:15.309
system that allows Utah County Justice Court to actually file charges

20:15.502 --> 20:19.725
and for us to actually issue citations for some of these city ordinance violations.

20:19.821 --> 20:22.904
You don't have that right now. The question is,

20:22.968 --> 20:26.196
who does that and how does that happen? And how do we create city ordinances

20:26.260 --> 20:30.177
to meet the needs of the citizens of the community? A couple community-oriented

20:30.677 --> 20:34.063
police officers now have the time and ability to work with Marcus and work with

20:34.191 --> 20:37.660
others to get these city ordinances on the MOT,

20:37.820 --> 20:41.031
the Master Offense Table, so it can be prosecuted

20:41.531 --> 20:45.041
in justice court. 2 proactive guys have the ability to

20:45.541 --> 20:49.194
do that. 2 proactive— I say guys, I mean law enforcement officers. 2 law enforcement

20:49.694 --> 20:53.460
officers can go into the communities. They can work with problem areas. Oftentimes you

20:53.960 --> 20:56.603
guys will hear up in, you know, from the citizens here about problems in their

20:57.103 --> 20:59.875
neighborhood, whether it be stop sign violations, speeders, and things like that, and that they

21:00.375 --> 21:03.627
don't see law enforcement there. Their complaint is valid.

21:04.060 --> 21:07.556
And sometimes we don't have the time to go into certain communities and

21:08.056 --> 21:11.595
to target traffic enforcement in these areas, or— and

21:12.095 --> 21:15.570
I say or because traffic enforcement is a temporary Band-Aid. What really needs to happen

21:16.070 --> 21:18.070
is a permanent fix. And you need a couple guys that can work with the

21:18.570 --> 21:22.093
citizens, that can work with our traffic engineers, that can work with

21:22.141 --> 21:25.410
people to permanently resolve the issues that are happening in these neighborhoods.

21:25.474 --> 21:29.417
Right. Whether that be through speed humps, you know, traffic design,

21:29.513 --> 21:31.693
whether that be putting up more stop signs or whatever.

21:32.959 --> 21:36.501
It's permanent solutions that you could have a couple law enforcement officers work

21:37.001 --> 21:40.548
on. And it's a way to get them to also help enforce

21:41.048 --> 21:44.718
those code violations or the traffic violations.

21:45.475 --> 21:48.870
And again, so we hear about it, and their complaints are legitimate.

21:50.150 --> 21:54.310
A couple proactive guys I think are going to make

21:54.810 --> 21:58.083
a huge impact in that area for Eagle Mountain right now. Eagle Mountain is lacking

21:58.131 --> 22:01.979
in that area where you— enough proactive time or finding enough officers to

22:02.479 --> 22:05.725
spend proactive time on it. I hope that answered your question.

22:05.822 --> 22:09.769
I think that's really helpful because as we look at budget and

22:10.269 --> 22:14.217
everything else, it's helpful for us to respond back to the residents and say,

22:14.538 --> 22:18.428
we've heard you and your concerns about X, Y, and Z, and one of

22:18.928 --> 22:21.997
the reasons why we're proposing this is because it will help us

22:22.093 --> 22:25.105
address X, Y, and Z. So that was helpful. Thank you.

22:25.997 --> 22:29.109
Every citizen in this community, not just Eagle Mountain, but I think everywhere

22:29.609 --> 22:33.204
around the country has an expected level of service from law enforcement. They expect something

22:33.269 --> 22:36.641
in their community. And as city council members, they're expecting you to kind of go

22:37.141 --> 22:39.291
to bat for that to make sure that we as law enforcement officers are taking

22:39.791 --> 22:43.000
care of problems in their city. And oftentimes you need

22:43.500 --> 22:46.341
somebody that can spend some time on that, not just a phone call and maybe

22:46.841 --> 22:49.906
stop by for 5 or 10 minutes and sic a traffic unit on it.

22:49.954 --> 22:53.712
That's not going to resolve the problem permanently. And I think full-time officers,

22:53.744 --> 22:56.923
community-oriented policing type officers, can go in there and help resolve

22:56.955 --> 23:00.340
these issues. So, I'll go on with this.

23:00.404 --> 23:05.665
So, based on the workload study, an increase of 11 to 13 sworn total is

23:06.002 --> 23:09.724
what we're looking at, what we need. Right now, we're too lean. We're not failing.

23:09.788 --> 23:13.478
We're not collapsing. We can still take calls. Eagle Mountain is

23:13.978 --> 23:16.510
a safe place. It continues to be a safe place.

23:17.553 --> 23:21.146
But if the manpower needs are not addressed, you run the risk of this turning

23:21.162 --> 23:24.596
into something that it shouldn't be. Thank you. Citizens should not have to call in

23:24.693 --> 23:28.722
and get an automated voicemail system about filing reports. They should

23:29.222 --> 23:32.800
be able to talk to somebody. And it's expected by the public. We don't want

23:33.300 --> 23:36.749
to turn into a city, a big city. I think that

23:37.249 --> 23:40.666
is not the direction Eagle Mountain wants to go. I think the citizens moved out

23:41.166 --> 23:44.278
here for a reason, and I think we should give them the proper level of

23:44.778 --> 23:48.179
law enforcement services. We're lean, and in this case,

23:48.292 --> 23:51.262
lean means fragile, and I think it's something we need to pay attention to.

23:51.823 --> 23:52.305
Thank you.

23:55.667 --> 23:57.302
Let me back up. I didn't read that last one to you.

23:59.350 --> 24:03.029
In short, based— shift— an estimate— this estimate

24:03.529 --> 24:08.145
is based on shift coverage requirements, realistic availability reductions,

24:08.178 --> 24:11.361
population size, geographical responsibility, and daily

24:11.393 --> 24:14.992
call volume, not on national averages. And why I put that in

24:15.492 --> 24:18.456
there is I just want to make it clear that in another slide later,

24:18.539 --> 24:21.952
I refer back to the officers per 1,000. I'm not doing that

24:22.001 --> 24:25.293
from a standpoint of you need to have 0.7 or

24:25.793 --> 24:29.394
you need to have 1 officer per 1,000. You don't. Eagle Mountain City does

24:29.458 --> 24:32.863
not need that number yet. Will it one day? I hope not.

24:33.088 --> 24:36.025
I hope you don't get to the point where crime requires you to have 1

24:36.073 --> 24:39.417
per 1,000, or the traffic here that may require that. Now,

24:39.465 --> 24:42.866
you're on track, you're growing, and— but I think

24:43.366 --> 24:44.841
we need to keep up with the needs of what you have right now.

24:45.359 --> 24:48.704
Do I think 0.7 is the number? No. But I'll tell you what I do

24:49.204 --> 24:52.634
think the number is here in a minute. Okay. This covers the 2 slides.

24:52.683 --> 24:56.346
This 2— these 2 slides cover the needs that I'm asking for. Um,

24:56.796 --> 25:00.652
I'm asking for 1 patrol deputy per team. That's 4

25:00.877 --> 25:04.782
for patrol deputies. I'm asking for 2 community

25:04.798 --> 25:08.719
police officers and a sergeant. Now, the sergeant will help supervise

25:08.751 --> 25:11.884
these community officers, but right now we have one of the sergeants. If you refer

25:12.384 --> 25:15.997
back and can go remember the slide I showed at first,

25:16.447 --> 25:20.494
That talked about Sergeant Max Morgan. Sergeant Max Morgan is— he's

25:20.703 --> 25:23.739
beyond span of control for the supervision, the job he needs to do. He has

25:23.835 --> 25:28.155
too many people underneath him. I mentioned we are also very lean on

25:28.268 --> 25:32.604
our administrative side of the house. We have plenty of patrol guys— or

25:33.104 --> 25:35.302
sorry, we have 68% of patrol guys,

25:35.880 --> 25:40.152
and which is according to the staffing, according to that ICMA

25:40.652 --> 25:42.369
study, it's above what the national average is.

25:44.445 --> 25:47.892
But we need to have one more per team to bring us up to

25:47.908 --> 25:51.160
5 instead of just 4. And then I need a sergeant,

25:51.710 --> 25:55.037
and Max needs some relief. He supervises too many

25:55.537 --> 25:58.678
people. And so my request is going to be for the 4 patrol deputies,

25:59.850 --> 26:04.043
the 2 community-oriented police deputies, and the one sergeant, for a total of 7.

26:04.301 --> 26:05.801
I mapped that out here just a little bit more,

26:07.865 --> 26:11.449
why I need the patrol sergeant. Yes. you know, current supervisory load exceeds best

26:11.949 --> 26:15.715
practices and help restore proper supervision leadership. Patrol deputies,

26:15.779 --> 26:19.740
asked for 6. The study recommends 8 to 10. Do I

26:20.240 --> 26:21.937
think we need 8 to 10? I do think we need 8 to 10.

26:22.386 --> 26:25.994
Financially, you know, I'm trying to be aware of, of, you know,

26:26.138 --> 26:29.297
at least be somewhat fiscally responsible of how Eagle

26:29.797 --> 26:32.312
Mountain stands right now and, and where you are as far as taxing and so

26:32.812 --> 26:36.449
forth. But you need 8 to 10. But I think right now on patrol deputies,

26:36.707 --> 26:40.321
An increase of 6 would bring

26:40.821 --> 26:44.619
us to a level that I think is doable. It brings

26:45.119 --> 26:48.387
us to safe staffing levels on patrol to ensure reliable response.

26:48.790 --> 26:52.349
The community officers can address repeat calls, neighborhood and business engagement,

26:52.397 --> 26:55.030
proactive enforcement, and reduce long-term demand on patrol.

26:56.430 --> 26:59.069
The last sentence kind of, Councilmember Clark,

26:59.279 --> 27:03.165
addresses what you were asking. Community-oriented policing strengthens trust between deputies

27:03.665 --> 27:06.671
and residents, proactively addressing neighborhood concerns,

27:06.704 --> 27:10.258
reduce crime through problem-solving partnership, and enhance overall public safety

27:10.758 --> 27:11.566
and quality of life in your community.

27:14.232 --> 27:18.070
Here's the cost breakdown. One sergeant, 6 deputies is going to cost

27:18.135 --> 27:20.807
an additional approximately $1.44 million.

27:22.905 --> 27:25.963
This is going to bring our saturation index to about 55 to 60%,

27:26.060 --> 27:29.441
slightly higher than that during the peak hours, but that's about

27:29.941 --> 27:33.207
where you're at. I think we'd call that fairly comfortable. It's doable

27:33.240 --> 27:33.520
for now.

27:36.426 --> 27:39.335
What that number brings us up to is 0.55 per residence.

27:40.547 --> 27:43.472
I still think we're below, but I think we're getting up there where we can

27:43.520 --> 27:47.100
be reasonably. We can talk more next year when it comes to budget requests and

27:47.600 --> 27:50.782
manpower requests, but I think 0.55 starts to bring us up to where Eagle Mountain

27:51.138 --> 27:56.023
is right now. Maybe to answer Councilmember Wood, your question of I

27:56.523 --> 27:59.487
don't think it's crime that's causing the big problem right now.

27:59.872 --> 28:02.758
Is crime going up? It is. But you have the number of residents that are

28:03.258 --> 28:06.832
growing up as well, are growing as well. So, but I think it brings it

28:07.332 --> 28:10.889
to a reasonable number. Again, Lehigh City's at 0.7. Saratoga Springs is 1.1.

28:10.937 --> 28:14.626
If you look around the national averages, they're higher than that. But if you

28:15.126 --> 28:17.240
look at some of the other cities that we've looked at in this county with

28:17.272 --> 28:20.881
Provo, Orem, they have a much bigger downtown, much bigger daytime traffic

28:21.381 --> 28:25.455
problem. But they're sitting at about 1, you know, 1.1 per 1,000

28:25.487 --> 28:28.817
residents as well. So do I think

28:29.317 --> 28:33.203
0.55 is high enough? Not yet. But it definitely

28:33.268 --> 28:36.272
gets us to a place where our guys can breathe a little bit and maybe

28:36.772 --> 28:42.758
do some more proactive stuff around Eagle Mountain. And that's needed. So I

28:43.258 --> 28:45.367
wanted to break this down because I know it's been brought up a couple times

28:45.867 --> 28:48.770
in our— in the city council. I just want to clarify some things in case

28:49.270 --> 28:51.207
you guys don't know this. I think you're probably aware of it, but I want

28:51.707 --> 28:53.935
to be clear on kind of how your contract monies are spent.

28:56.320 --> 28:59.901
The average deputy, and the way the contract is sent out is we

28:59.998 --> 29:03.369
basically take the lowest-end deputy and the highest-end deputy and we say what's

29:03.869 --> 29:06.940
the average that they're making, and then we times that by the number of deputies

29:06.989 --> 29:10.254
that are working out here. So the average cost for the deputy out here in

29:10.754 --> 29:12.626
your contract with benefits and salaries about $140,000.

29:13.906 --> 29:17.074
$40,000. And then on top of that, you're getting billed for the motor

29:17.574 --> 29:21.434
pool part portion of it. Utah County bears

29:21.466 --> 29:25.337
the responsibility of spending the money to purchase those cars. Eagle Mountain spends

29:25.370 --> 29:29.241
the money to basically pay the recapitalization costs on those over the next 4 years.

29:29.306 --> 29:32.367
We trade those cars in to get new vehicles. Some of that

29:32.867 --> 29:36.957
equipment stays with the vehicle, a lot of it doesn't. Radios typically stay, light bars

29:36.974 --> 29:40.778
will stay for an extra cycle on that rotation. But they

29:40.794 --> 29:43.983
also need to be rotated out eventually as well. They have radar equipment inside the

29:44.483 --> 29:47.620
vehicles, video equipment. The deputy themselves has body cams and some other

29:48.120 --> 29:52.027
stuff. So it breaks it down what all of those costs are that are most

29:52.527 --> 29:55.777
of the cops costs that are that are apply on that contract.

29:56.178 --> 29:58.982
The fourth bullet point talks about administrative costs.

29:59.463 --> 30:03.133
You deal with your HR costs, evidence, crime labs, crime scene costs,

30:03.149 --> 30:06.244
records bureau. That's all lumped into kind of your overhead costs as part of that

30:06.276 --> 30:09.584
contract. And also they cover

30:09.744 --> 30:13.020
our crime reporting system, which is Spillman, our liability insurance

30:13.520 --> 30:17.163
per deputy. That's required by law. Most states or most agencies

30:17.211 --> 30:21.851
around the state are paying that. I believe that's about $1,100

30:22.012 --> 30:25.287
a year that every single officer and deputy in

30:25.787 --> 30:29.173
the state has to cover. And the cities and counties are paying that fee,

30:29.558 --> 30:32.995
but it's per deputy. Is what they're paying for. That's required. And then

30:33.495 --> 30:36.946
of course there's a, there's a little bit of that money that goes towards SWAT

30:37.446 --> 30:40.625
response, SAR response, canine. For those of you keeping up

30:41.125 --> 30:43.291
here, you know, we had a, we had a big call down at the new

30:43.791 --> 30:47.403
QTS building this last Friday of a bomb scare. You had, I think,

30:47.451 --> 30:51.226
6 canines from around the county, including other

30:51.242 --> 30:54.438
agencies, and, uh, and then you had other personnel that responded

30:54.938 --> 30:58.010
on that call. And a lot of those fees cover some of those response from

30:58.510 --> 31:01.570
those entity. You also have drone, which is part of that. We do have

31:02.070 --> 31:05.800
a full-time drone guy working out here that's part of the drone team.

31:05.890 --> 31:09.370
And so that's all part of that service. So total cost per deputy is

31:09.870 --> 31:12.920
about $200,000, and the sergeant's about $247,000,

31:13.090 --> 31:17.210
$240,000. That's where the number came up, $1.44 million,

31:17.710 --> 31:23.597
was basically that cost of the 6 deputies along with the 1 sergeant. So From

31:24.097 --> 31:27.690
the sheriff, he—you know—I've had a lot of conversation

31:28.190 --> 31:30.996
with him about Eagle Mountain and the contract.

31:32.087 --> 31:36.645
He values this contract. He values the citizens of Eagle Mountain,

31:37.143 --> 31:41.252
and he has an expectation of the service

31:41.752 --> 31:45.633
that we're expected to provide to the citizens. But more importantly, he hears quite often

31:45.666 --> 31:48.796
the expected level of service the citizens. Want in their community.

31:49.342 --> 31:52.649
He was here last night. He spoke with the citizens of Eagle Mountain. They have

31:53.149 --> 31:56.551
an expectation. They voted you in to kind of meet those expectations.

31:56.969 --> 32:00.405
And right now, I think we're short. We're coming up short on

32:00.437 --> 32:04.098
the level of service we want to provide the citizens of Eagle Mountain.

32:04.467 --> 32:08.144
And I think adding some of these deputies, these 7 staffing

32:08.224 --> 32:12.239
plan requests, gets us closer

32:12.739 --> 32:16.054
to where we need to be. And so we talk about wants and needs.

32:16.951 --> 32:19.964
This is a need. This isn't a want. I think the want, we get into

32:20.557 --> 32:24.226
the higher numbers. But right now it's a need. For me personally,

32:24.274 --> 32:27.784
my question to you is, is what do we— what do you— what

32:28.284 --> 32:31.854
do the citizens of Eagle Mountain value? And when you talk about increasing

32:32.354 --> 32:34.882
our staffing plan for law enforcement services, what type of law enforcement do you want?

32:34.930 --> 32:38.408
What do you value? And we want to provide the best service

32:38.504 --> 32:41.766
for you guys and And it's tied directly to staffing.

32:43.490 --> 32:46.863
We have some of the best, and in case there's other cities

32:46.896 --> 32:49.305
listening to this, which they're probably not, but I'll tell you, I think we got

32:49.805 --> 32:53.210
the best group of deputies in this county, if not the state and around

32:53.710 --> 32:56.384
the country. And they do an amazing job.

32:57.673 --> 33:01.024
They're young, they're motivated, they love being out here, being part of Eagle Mountain,

33:01.443 --> 33:05.053
and the level of service they're providing is incredible,

33:05.135 --> 33:08.263
I think. I think if you look at Google reviews, we do okay.

33:09.460 --> 33:12.940
So again, that's at the

33:12.956 --> 33:16.259
very end. I wrote, let's keep Eagle Mountain a great place to live.

33:18.060 --> 33:21.348
I think, I think it's a phenomenal place,

33:22.110 --> 33:25.593
and I think the citizens have an expectation. And, and that's kind

33:26.093 --> 33:27.925
of my presentation. I put on one more slide. I'm going to click to it

33:28.523 --> 33:32.307
because a discussion point came up about— I think, I think it was Councilmember

33:32.807 --> 33:36.723
Clark that asked me. a while ago about the impact of big businesses

33:36.788 --> 33:40.091
coming here. And you have a couple more big businesses coming here, but you just

33:40.107 --> 33:43.943
had a big business open up its doors in Walmart. And so

33:43.991 --> 33:48.586
I thought, you know, can I gauge how this affects law enforcement?

33:48.715 --> 33:52.806
I can only run stats, and I can tell you from stats, is there an

33:53.306 --> 33:57.344
impact on law enforcement services provided to Eagle Mountain? Does this impact our

33:57.844 --> 34:01.112
law enforcement needs? Yes. So just for fun, I put together this. I wanted to

34:01.612 --> 34:05.088
go through in 2025, quarter 1, 2, 3, and 4. So it's just, you know,

34:05.104 --> 34:07.637
January, February, March, quarter 1, and then we go on from there.

34:08.551 --> 34:11.645
This is in a grid surrounding— this is not calls

34:11.677 --> 34:15.172
for service to Walmart. This is in a grid where Walmart is located.

34:15.669 --> 34:19.580
I simply said, how many police incidents did we have in this grid in

34:19.612 --> 34:23.075
the first quarter of 2025? We had 61 or 62.

34:23.139 --> 34:26.076
That can be traffic stops. That's not a lot. That can be traffic accidents,

34:26.172 --> 34:29.448
that can be calls for service, theft, or anything. But in that grid, this is

34:29.705 --> 34:32.498
our grid that we use in our law enforcement mapping system.

34:33.092 --> 34:36.577
We had 62 in quarter 2, we had 63 in quarter 3

34:36.609 --> 34:39.884
when Walmart opened on August 27th, we had 108, and in

34:39.900 --> 34:43.593
quarter 4 we had 141. That is not a reflection

34:44.093 --> 34:46.869
on what Walmart is doing good or bad. It's simply the impact that it has

34:46.901 --> 34:51.686
there on law enforcement and all calls for service in that specific

34:52.186 --> 34:56.148
specific area. Um, between Q1 of 2025 and Q1

34:56.164 --> 35:00.289
of 2026, there's a 73% increase. It's one portion

35:00.789 --> 35:04.706
of the city. Um, this does not mean things are going downhill in that area.

35:04.755 --> 35:08.364
It's not. It's just simply more traffic, more people doing more things.

35:08.980 --> 35:11.719
It's impacting us when, when, uh,

35:12.368 --> 35:15.610
when new business comes here like a Lowe's, a Smith's,

35:17.196 --> 35:20.542
In the south end of the city, you're— it's going to impact us. It's going

35:21.042 --> 35:24.599
to impact our law enforcement and our ability to respond effectively

35:25.504 --> 35:28.730
in times of need. We can handle all the traffic accidents in the world,

35:28.746 --> 35:31.323
but when you have a Part 1 or Part 2 crime or a Priority 1

35:31.823 --> 35:35.221
or Priority 2 crime, we get tied up and our response times become slower.

35:36.150 --> 35:38.863
So again, I'll leave with you the thought of,

35:39.270 --> 35:42.113
of, uh, there is a want. Yes.

35:42.652 --> 35:45.757
And I want more guys. I want a lot more guys.

35:46.097 --> 35:49.494
But there's a need, and 7 almost meets

35:49.994 --> 35:53.560
that need. At minimum, I think

35:54.060 --> 35:57.221
a 7 is a reasonable request for the residents

35:57.302 --> 36:01.077
of Eagle Mountain. Very reasonable. What they

36:01.577 --> 36:04.883
need is more, but 7 is a reasonable request.

36:06.335 --> 36:10.021
So, questions? Yes. So if a backup unit comes from

36:10.521 --> 36:13.755
another city, who pays for that?

36:14.171 --> 36:18.017
Are we— is that time tracked and then— No,

36:18.658 --> 36:21.879
it's tracked on data. It's not tracked on payment. Each city,

36:22.088 --> 36:25.004
it's part of just kind of an interlocal agreement. It's nothing signed.

36:25.068 --> 36:29.139
They don't sign an agreement between other agencies. It's just what

36:29.639 --> 36:32.888
they do. And we do it in reverse sometimes.

36:33.883 --> 36:37.626
There's a difference between Utah County Patrol units and Eagle Mountain

36:37.883 --> 36:41.434
Utah County Patrol units. We're 2 different agencies essentially, even though we both work with

36:41.934 --> 36:45.065
the Sheriff's Office. We handle calls here, but occasionally you get a call out in

36:45.565 --> 36:48.391
Five Mile Pass and we're the closest person there and there's somebody with a life-threatening

36:48.423 --> 36:51.315
injury. We're going to go on that if we're available. We get there, we don't

36:51.815 --> 36:54.657
take the call, we just handle the emergency portion of the call. Utah County deputies

36:54.754 --> 36:58.626
will show up and handle the actual call. They will spend the hours on report

36:59.126 --> 37:02.672
writing, a follow-up, going to the hospital. arresting somebody if need be and booking them

37:02.721 --> 37:05.113
into jail. We won't do that. We will just simply go out there to help.

37:05.594 --> 37:08.709
Saratoga will do the same for us quite often. We'll get backed up on calls,

37:08.805 --> 37:11.470
we'll need a backup officer, and they'll come into our city and they'll assist with

37:11.970 --> 37:15.644
backup. That is not a payment thing. We don't— Eagle Mountain or Saratoga wouldn't charge

37:16.144 --> 37:19.256
us, and, uh, and when we do that for Saratoga, we won't charge them.

37:19.593 --> 37:23.591
It's just an understanding between the 2 agencies. And then a question on the

37:23.896 --> 37:27.523
animal control. So when your officer goes out on animal control, Someone's animal

37:28.023 --> 37:31.657
misbehaved, is there a fine that's getting issued to that

37:32.554 --> 37:36.176
animal owner to compensate for the time that we

37:36.676 --> 37:40.807
need to spend for your services there? No, there's not a— there's

37:41.307 --> 37:44.621
a citation that can be issued, but we don't charge them on an animal control

37:44.669 --> 37:47.938
call. We go out and handle the call. Now, if the animal gets placed in

37:48.438 --> 37:51.384
some— one of the animal shelters, they're going to have to pay a fee to

37:51.884 --> 37:53.729
get the animal out, but there's not a additional charge for them to come out

37:54.229 --> 37:57.110
there and handle an animal control call. Unless a citation is issued,

37:57.351 --> 38:01.236
then they're gonna have to pay that fine for the citation. Is your animal

38:01.736 --> 38:05.214
control officer also a law enforcement officer? They're part of the patrol numbers

38:05.279 --> 38:08.772
we gave you. Yeah, so you have— Utah County changed that

38:09.272 --> 38:12.454
some years ago. Animal control used to be just— it was a— it was still

38:12.648 --> 38:15.716
a law enforcement officer went on duty, but they

38:16.216 --> 38:19.913
were paid less and only handled animal control calls. It changed many

38:19.929 --> 38:22.898
years ago. Now for us out in Eagle Mountain, we have 2 animal control officers,

38:22.914 --> 38:26.252
but they are patrol deputies. They're full-fledged patrol deputies, and when things get busy,

38:26.284 --> 38:30.103
they handle patrol calls. But in Eagle Mountain— Eagle Mountain has

38:30.296 --> 38:34.003
its fair share of animal calls. And so we

38:34.067 --> 38:37.293
warrant 2. We probably could warrant— this would be a want.

38:37.662 --> 38:40.374
Yeah. Could we deal with another animal control guy? Absolutely.

38:40.888 --> 38:44.820
We have 2. We're trying to get them set up so they work basically 7

38:45.320 --> 38:48.951
days a week. Not 24/7, but we try to keep them in that

38:49.015 --> 38:52.577
Monday through Sunday. We want them on duty at some point just because

38:53.077 --> 38:55.747
of the amount of animal control calls we get here. There's a lot.

38:58.037 --> 39:01.783
So— Can you go back to that first slide that, uh, it was one

39:02.283 --> 39:06.227
of the first slides that kind of showed that breakout of— yeah,

39:06.309 --> 39:09.804
essentially. And you know the animal control

39:10.304 --> 39:13.813
guys. I think Jim Bingham one of our longtime— he's—

39:13.877 --> 39:17.227
so he's the ACO in that chart. And so,

39:17.243 --> 39:20.288
Jim— yeah, Jim's been around for a long time. We have another guy named Walkenshaw.

39:20.545 --> 39:24.055
Unfortunately, Deputy Walkenshaw came here a couple years ago,

39:24.856 --> 39:27.373
and, uh, or a year ago, um,

39:27.806 --> 39:30.755
he's now putting in his 2-week notice. He's going to go to Salt Lake City,

39:31.027 --> 39:34.730
and so we'll be short on animal control until we get that filled. Um,

39:35.419 --> 39:38.785
he's absolutely a great guy, but he, he received a great opportunity to go up

39:39.285 --> 39:43.088
to County north, and Salt Lake City is going to pay him

39:43.104 --> 39:46.697
accordingly. And so we're going to lose him soon,

39:46.875 --> 39:50.111
which is unfortunate, but we'll get hopefully a replacement for him soon,

39:50.225 --> 39:52.473
get us back full-staffed again.

39:54.770 --> 39:56.602
Okay, there's the slide. You have a question, Mayor?

39:58.648 --> 40:01.321
I didn't particularly. I just wanted them to see kind of what this was going

40:01.821 --> 40:05.149
to be. So these 4 to the right, he's asking for a

40:05.649 --> 40:08.760
5th guy on each one of those blue slides. You got a sergeant, a 5th

40:08.825 --> 40:12.821
guy. Max Morgan's on the left. So splitting— maybe

40:13.321 --> 40:17.218
you could explain how you split that. So 2 SROs under each

40:18.310 --> 40:21.873
sergeant with a new— and then 1 ACO under each

40:22.001 --> 40:25.500
and 1 traffic, or what's— We would leave them as groups together. So Max

40:26.000 --> 40:29.544
would probably continue to do the SRO and the ACO. The goal is to take

40:30.044 --> 40:33.133
a community police officer and and the traffic, they sometimes can work

40:33.213 --> 40:36.372
real well hand in hand. Because a lot of times, the issues we're dealing with

40:36.872 --> 40:40.777
in neighborhoods are traffic enforcement issues. And if you assign our 2 community-oriented officers

40:40.890 --> 40:44.206
to deal with— to work with citizens in the community, and if they need

40:44.706 --> 40:47.943
to utilize the traffic unit, they can each go through

40:48.443 --> 40:52.378
their same sergeant. So typically, it'll be 1 sergeant over 2 COPs

40:52.668 --> 40:55.909
and 2 traffic units, is ideally what we'd like to

40:56.409 --> 40:58.941
see. Thank you. Chief, thanks for this information.

41:00.337 --> 41:03.738
How long have you worked in Eagle Mountain or kind of overseeing our—

41:04.669 --> 41:08.070
Well, that's a trick question. No, it's not a trick question. I, I was

41:08.570 --> 41:10.781
assigned out here a long time ago as a sergeant. Corey Ride and I were

41:11.281 --> 41:14.776
sergeants together way back in the day. We were opposing shifts as the 2 sergeants

41:15.276 --> 41:18.563
out here. Then I left after a while, and I've been out

41:19.063 --> 41:23.885
here since January of last year. Okay, so just a year and a Okay,

41:23.950 --> 41:27.285
that benchmarking data is really helpful for me to just understand

41:27.350 --> 41:31.380
where other cities sit for deputy

41:31.880 --> 41:36.364
or sergeant— I'm not going to get the language right— deputies per population.

41:37.120 --> 41:40.476
I'm curious if that number has changed over time or has

41:40.976 --> 41:43.962
it been getting worse? Was there a point in the past where we were at

41:44.462 --> 41:48.359
0.7 and we just have not been adequately funding our

41:48.969 --> 41:52.018
Sheriff's Office, and we're— it's getting to

41:52.518 --> 41:55.501
where we're early on. Yeah, like if we're just super thin and tight on the

41:56.001 --> 41:59.465
budget. When the first— when the city was first created, we had

41:59.481 --> 42:02.595
some deputies that would come out and take calls, and eventually we started to assign

42:02.691 --> 42:06.158
people out here. Eventually you got a sergeant, then you had 2 sergeants, then pretty

42:06.658 --> 42:10.250
soon you had 3 sergeants, administrative sergeant, and you always had a lieutenant

42:10.750 --> 42:13.636
over it. And, and then you had deputies that were assigned out here,

42:13.765 --> 42:17.701
but At first, it was probably

42:18.201 --> 42:21.831
close to 0.7, 0.8 way back in the late '90s, early 2000s.

42:22.280 --> 42:25.632
Early on. But then that started to change. And what

42:26.003 --> 42:29.149
happened really is over the last couple years, I think it's in 2 years you

42:29.649 --> 42:33.167
increased by 17,000 or 18,000 in population. And then more

42:33.667 --> 42:37.142
than that as you went back further. You can still see, I think the increase

42:37.642 --> 42:40.996
is about 6-point-something, 7%. population increase over the last

42:41.496 --> 42:45.152
3 years. And, and I think because of budgeting issues,

42:45.168 --> 42:48.682
and I very understandably so, um, that number's gone down. So you

42:49.182 --> 42:52.630
dwindle down. Uh, when I first got here, I think we were at about 0.49,

42:52.646 --> 42:55.678
0.5 last year. And then, um,

42:55.839 --> 42:58.920
with— because of shortages on requests, it went down from there,

42:59.530 --> 43:04.006
and now we're at 0.45. Yeah, okay. Yeah. And so that— I

43:04.506 --> 43:06.301
made a comment about, you know, we don't want to become a big city.

43:06.349 --> 43:09.658
What happens if If you're not careful and we continue

43:10.158 --> 43:11.635
to drop like that, a couple things are going to happen. You're going to have

43:11.651 --> 43:14.013
sticker shock to the citizens when you tell them we have to, we don't have

43:14.045 --> 43:17.757
a choice. And you're going to have to increase manpower.

43:18.078 --> 43:22.063
The worst-case scenario, tragedy happens, which causes cities to then

43:22.898 --> 43:26.192
throw a lot of money at law enforcement. I think every chief

43:26.692 --> 43:29.390
in the country will say we don't want something tragic to happen before the city

43:29.890 --> 43:32.989
council takes action. We would like to see action taken before tragedy strikes. Thank you.

43:34.109 --> 43:37.345
And then we don't

43:37.828 --> 43:41.468
want you to turn into a city. If you ignore the numbers

43:42.418 --> 43:46.040
of law enforcement officers needed in a city, pretty soon you do run into the

43:46.540 --> 43:50.275
risk of becoming that big city that you don't want. You don't

43:50.291 --> 43:52.996
want the crime to start to take over. You don't want people to be,

43:53.592 --> 43:57.230
you know, doing things in communities that disrupt

43:57.471 --> 44:00.976
your livability. You don't want your quality of life to diminish in the,

44:01.121 --> 44:04.264
in the areas that you're living in. And the people that are moving here,

44:04.409 --> 44:08.036
they have an expectation of quality of life. And if we're not careful on the—

44:08.294 --> 44:12.195
not paying attention to the number of officers you need in the city, that'll diminish.

44:12.551 --> 44:15.059
And we don't want that to go down. We want quality of life to either

44:15.559 --> 44:18.763
to stay or get better. So thank you. Yep.

44:23.421 --> 44:24.666
You were a lot tougher last time.

44:26.779 --> 44:30.633
I already beat you up. You did. You did. Make no

44:31.133 --> 44:34.872
mistake. I've— Chief, I had to ask him if he needed a cry room

44:34.968 --> 44:38.388
for a minute. It wasn't that

44:38.436 --> 44:41.889
bad. Yes, it was. But no, it wasn't

44:42.389 --> 44:45.565
bad at all. But your questions, Mayor, are great. All of your questions are great.

44:46.047 --> 44:49.820
And if you have more, I'm happy to answer them. Again, I'll stick

44:50.320 --> 44:52.950
back to the comment I made before. Is, is there are needs and there's wants,

44:52.966 --> 44:57.057
and we know what that means. Um, this is no longer a want. The city

44:57.557 --> 45:01.453
needs it and needs it desperately. So can I, can I just share a

45:01.953 --> 45:05.640
comment, not, not a question, uh, just an observation that

45:06.140 --> 45:08.977
I've had living in Eagle Mountain for almost 20 years?

45:10.228 --> 45:14.848
Our, our property taxes have not increased

45:15.348 --> 45:18.844
from, from the city. We, we have not kept up. So the property taxes

45:18.860 --> 45:21.992
that I pay today on my house are the same as what they were 10

45:22.492 --> 45:25.669
years ago. But the cost per deputy

45:25.862 --> 45:29.330
that covers my neighborhood, that has increased.

45:29.957 --> 45:33.297
And so while people see populations coming in and new growth,

45:34.132 --> 45:37.456
that doesn't really keep up for the fact that the property tax

45:37.504 --> 45:40.041
has not been increasing on a lot of homes in Eagle Mountain.

45:40.635 --> 45:43.911
And I don't think a lot of citizens really recognize that. And so this ratio,

45:43.927 --> 45:47.330
what you're telling— what you just shared with us is we probably started out at

45:47.539 --> 45:50.748
a 0.7% coverage ratio.

45:50.812 --> 45:53.524
That's what I'm going to call it. Now we're at 0.45%,

45:54.358 --> 45:56.621
and because we have not, um,

45:57.327 --> 46:00.712
kept up with the cost of inflation, we are— we're now behind.

46:00.777 --> 46:03.713
Now we're getting to the point where it's like we need to hire 7,

46:04.868 --> 46:08.639
you know, deputy sergeants. Like, it's just going to continue

46:09.139 --> 46:12.057
to get worse unless we really address the fact. And I would agree, this is

46:12.105 --> 46:15.642
a need, not a not a want. Like, safety in our community

46:16.307 --> 46:19.861
is probably the highest priority I think that I have. Roads,

46:20.151 --> 46:24.018
safety, those are things that governments should be addressing, and I think that's what our

46:24.098 --> 46:27.481
citizens kind of expect us to be watching. So I appreciate your

46:27.626 --> 46:30.798
presentation. Can I get a copy of this? Absolutely.

46:30.878 --> 46:33.850
Okay, thank you. Matter of fact, I'll email it to all of you as soon

46:34.350 --> 46:37.063
as we're done. Just to kind of piggyback on that, I want to give you

46:37.563 --> 46:40.857
a little context, and I know Chief's You know, when McDowell was here earlier and

46:41.357 --> 46:45.129
probably right around your first year-ish, Colby and Carolyn were still on council.

46:46.058 --> 46:49.492
We were at the 1 per 2,200 and we increased that year.

46:49.863 --> 46:52.137
Just out of my head, I came up with a number of 1,750,

46:53.055 --> 46:56.887
which both McDowell and I think Knutson agrees that today is a pretty,

46:57.564 --> 46:58.787
that gets us to that 0.65-ish,

47:01.000 --> 47:04.287
0.64 was it, I can't remember, per 1,000,

47:04.303 --> 47:07.371
right? Yeah. And we got there. The problem is, is the next day we built

47:07.871 --> 47:12.171
another house. So, um, it's only been 4

47:12.671 --> 47:16.040
or 5 years when we, we got to that number. Then the second part though

47:16.540 --> 47:20.295
that I think is interesting, because our citizens say, well, you're adding— you're

47:20.795 --> 47:24.228
increasing my taxes because we have to add officers because of growth. But what you

47:24.728 --> 47:27.728
pointed out was that your taxes haven't gone up forever, and inflation.

47:28.482 --> 47:31.756
So I think the difference is that same officer the last time

47:32.256 --> 47:35.602
we raised taxes, like 2003 I believe, which we'll learn from

47:36.102 --> 47:39.545
Kimberly, is paid more today. So you're not increasing taxes

47:40.045 --> 47:42.237
to pay for more officers, you're increasing to pay for the one that's still covering

47:42.253 --> 47:46.115
your house, and the new people are paying more to add to the

47:46.615 --> 47:49.721
other 7. So kind of the context on, on how that works.

47:49.801 --> 47:53.166
So Brett had a comment and we'll come right back. Yeah, just, just,

47:53.262 --> 47:56.387
just some thoughts. I mean, I've been here a couple of years now, been through—

47:56.515 --> 48:00.673
this is This is my 5th budget season, and it seems like every year that

48:01.107 --> 48:03.307
this is a topic that we come to and that we address.

48:04.720 --> 48:07.931
And we've really tried to be sensitive, Councilmember Pugh, to make sure that

48:08.431 --> 48:11.833
we're giving them what we can, but within budget constraints. And I think—

48:12.122 --> 48:16.618
so the process of that is— and I remember Chief

48:16.666 --> 48:19.925
McDowell standing there and Mayor Gray at the time on the council,

48:19.973 --> 48:22.815
and we had this very robust debate and discussion discussion about,

48:23.184 --> 48:26.345
well, what's the number? And I don't remember the exact number that he asked for,

48:26.794 --> 48:29.826
but we had a discussion and we, we added 2 more to the budget,

48:29.907 --> 48:33.533
2 more than Chief McDowell asked for, because it is a priority for

48:34.033 --> 48:37.769
us. The problem comes from the funding mechanism is not keeping up

48:38.269 --> 48:41.202
with the demand. And so our citizens, they say,

48:41.298 --> 48:44.058
well, but you're adding all of these new people who should be paying for that.

48:44.122 --> 48:46.978
And they are, but they're paying for it at a rate that's 20 years old.

48:47.732 --> 48:50.829
That's the problem. And so we're having to make up that shortfall

48:51.262 --> 48:54.675
from other funding sources. And so everything suffers.

48:55.108 --> 48:58.681
The, the Sheriff's Department suffers in their personnel. So does our city

48:58.713 --> 49:01.854
staff. And our ability to meet the basic needs of the

49:02.354 --> 49:06.646
city are becoming more difficult, not less difficult. And, and so I

49:07.146 --> 49:09.594
just— I mean, here's what's going through my mind and some of the questions I

49:10.094 --> 49:12.975
wrote down. I thought, he wants 7, what can we do to provide 8 to

49:13.475 --> 49:16.597
10? I'd really like to know, how can we modify this year's budget to give

49:17.097 --> 49:20.428
him 8 to 10 that he really needs. And I don't know what that means

49:20.928 --> 49:23.890
or where we'd have to cut. And that's probably what we're looking at if we

49:24.390 --> 49:28.750
choose to do something like that, is where are we going to claw back $600,000

49:29.250 --> 49:32.767
if we're going to add 3 more deputies beyond the 7 he's requesting? And then

49:32.783 --> 49:36.373
how are we going to fund it? Because it's an ongoing cost and expense.

49:36.438 --> 49:39.565
And I know, I know Kimberly's going to talk to us a little bit about

49:40.065 --> 49:43.298
that, but that's the seriousness and the weightiness of what we're really dealing with.

49:44.196 --> 49:47.483
And now having been here 5 years, I'm understanding this a little better than I

49:47.983 --> 49:51.492
did in year 1, perhaps year 2. And being a property tax

49:51.992 --> 49:55.292
owner and, and, and an owner of a rental home in Eagle

49:55.792 --> 49:59.510
Mountain, I don't want to see my property taxes go up. But they haven't

50:00.010 --> 50:03.166
for 22 years while I've been here. They have for

50:03.262 --> 50:06.854
every other taxing entity but the City of Eagle Mountain, every one of

50:06.887 --> 50:10.334
them. I mean, they've gone up significantly. Because they've needed

50:10.834 --> 50:13.957
to, to meet their needs, and they haven't postponed that like we have. So I

50:14.005 --> 50:17.436
think, I think there's some room for growth and discussion, but that's the question I'm

50:17.936 --> 50:21.059
going to ask each of you to consider, is what would it take to get

50:21.559 --> 50:24.666
to the 8 to 10 that he really thinks he needs to provide that

50:25.166 --> 50:28.097
level of service? Because if we're not providing adequate policing services, why are we sitting

50:28.273 --> 50:31.720
here? Well, that's kind

50:32.220 --> 50:34.060
of along the lines of where I'm heading. I don't think this is going to

50:34.560 --> 50:38.058
be a surprise to you. I'm disappointed

50:38.558 --> 50:41.527
that we have not built this into our system, frankly.

50:41.783 --> 50:44.979
I mean, I think as a citizen— I'm not going to speak as a city

50:45.479 --> 50:49.203
councilmember— as a citizen, there's an expectation that I have of public safety,

50:49.877 --> 50:51.933
and that's the number one thing for my family.

50:52.591 --> 50:56.124
Roads, water, sewer— that all comes after the fact that

50:56.624 --> 50:59.047
I want to know that my kids can play in my front yard without having

50:59.547 --> 51:01.376
to worry about me sitting in the front yard to make sure that they're going

51:01.876 --> 51:05.633
to be safe the time. And I think that that's what our citizens expect.

51:06.183 --> 51:09.494
And I, I don't mean for this to sound negative. It's gonna sound

51:09.994 --> 51:12.628
negative, but this is my 3rd budget season now,

51:12.952 --> 51:15.621
and it seems like public safety is an afterthought every time.

51:17.287 --> 51:20.765
To me, and I'm hearing this from the citizens, this is

51:20.862 --> 51:24.562
the first priority. This has to be the very first

51:25.062 --> 51:28.384
thing on our list of things to take care of And then we're gonna have

51:28.416 --> 51:31.870
to make everything else work. To Brett's point, I agree,

51:32.175 --> 51:35.308
8 to 10 is what we should be shooting for. It's not even

51:35.808 --> 51:37.724
an excess, it's just to get it to baseline.

51:39.594 --> 51:42.421
I don't think that's an unreasonable expectation by our citizens.

51:43.385 --> 51:47.707
So I agree, I think it's something that we need to, this budget,

51:48.109 --> 51:50.810
figure out a way to do that. But we need to put a mechanism in

51:50.923 --> 51:54.634
place. And if that is a tax increase, Then we need to

51:55.134 --> 51:58.898
tell the citizens this is the reason why. Yes, we're increasing. I've watched

51:59.398 --> 52:02.601
this budget discussion Saratoga Springs is having online right

52:02.633 --> 52:07.026
now, and it really is kind of laughable, actually, how naive

52:07.154 --> 52:09.398
citizens have become about how things are paid for.

52:10.729 --> 52:14.256
Yes, you're increasing number of households, but your liability goes up,

52:14.593 --> 52:18.151
your public safety goes up, and that's just to cover your house

52:18.264 --> 52:21.636
that you just built. That doesn't account for the fact that the people

52:22.136 --> 52:25.444
have to be paid a living wage that's commensurate with what that job requires.

52:26.553 --> 52:30.072
And to Zach's point, or Brett maybe, I can't remember

52:30.572 --> 52:34.314
which one of you said that, we're paying it based on a 2003. That's ridiculous.

52:35.567 --> 52:39.970
And so we get sticker shock every time we see $250,000.

52:40.051 --> 52:43.361
We're like, well, God, we can't, that's $1.5 million. Yeah,

52:43.988 --> 52:47.424
that's what it costs. There's different equipment that's available now. They're not

52:47.924 --> 52:51.631
just walking around with a radio and a gun anymore. They have equipment

52:51.679 --> 52:55.661
that they're wearing on themselves. They have equipment that's in the car that

52:55.870 --> 52:59.772
records and provides safety for them, but also when it goes to

52:59.788 --> 53:02.196
court so that the person that is being arrested,

53:03.256 --> 53:06.660
that their rights are being protected, but also the law

53:06.676 --> 53:09.486
enforcement officer that's doing the arresting is being protected.

53:11.737 --> 53:15.629
I— we can't keep having this discussion like this is an afterthought. It's— to

53:16.129 --> 53:18.701
me, the mechanism needs to be put in place by us,

53:20.322 --> 53:23.785
the policymakers. It can't just be approving a budget. We need

53:24.285 --> 53:28.117
to put a policy in place that as we grow, we're going to increase

53:28.133 --> 53:30.261
by this many officers every single year.

53:31.342 --> 53:35.216
And if it becomes to a point where the contract is now too

53:35.716 --> 53:38.621
expensive for us to do, then we evaluate at that time. But at that point,

53:39.138 --> 53:41.799
we should be having that discussion of how do we get to the number we

53:42.299 --> 53:45.331
should be at. If we're supposed to be at 48 officers and we're at 20—

53:45.927 --> 53:48.723
what'd you say the number was right now? We're at 35 total.

53:48.965 --> 53:52.288
35 total. And how many sworn? That's at 35 sworn.

53:52.368 --> 53:55.756
35 sworn. So we're already deficient by 13 of where we ought

53:56.256 --> 54:01.562
to be. I just— we

54:02.062 --> 54:05.854
need to do a public relations program

54:06.354 --> 54:08.464
to get out to our citizens and let them know, number one, what the cost

54:08.513 --> 54:10.977
of this lifestyle that they enjoy is.

54:11.793 --> 54:14.947
That's number one. And public safety needs to be at the very beginning of that

54:15.447 --> 54:18.586
discussion. Number two, there is

54:19.086 --> 54:22.912
a cost associated with living a peaceful life, and it is

54:23.008 --> 54:26.680
the taxes that we pay. Our taxes from

54:26.728 --> 54:30.158
Eagle Mountain haven't gone up, and this misnomer that our taxes keep going

54:30.271 --> 54:33.508
up is caused by other agencies. And yes, that's part of

54:34.008 --> 54:35.804
life too. You have to pay for schools and you have to pay for the

54:36.304 --> 54:39.433
fire services that we have and for the county. They will continue to go up,

54:39.497 --> 54:42.965
yet we continue to not raise our taxes and our costs are going through the

54:43.465 --> 54:46.642
roof. We're looking at the same type of problem that we had with our sewer

54:47.142 --> 54:50.239
and our water, and we're acting like we can just keep band-aiding this.

54:51.379 --> 54:53.723
I, I think the time has passed for us to do that, and we need

54:54.223 --> 54:57.697
to be serious about this as we were about raising the fees for our water,

54:57.826 --> 55:01.795
raising the fees for our sewer. We have to fund our public safety.

55:05.740 --> 55:09.334
Excuse me. I wholeheartedly agree with all of the comments

55:09.834 --> 55:13.382
that have been made by the council. I mean, police and public safety

55:13.882 --> 55:17.329
services are our essential core as far as you look at

55:17.829 --> 55:21.226
our model and the essential services we provide and the quality

55:21.726 --> 55:25.702
of life we provide. provide and the economic prosperity provide,

55:25.831 --> 55:29.113
all of that's built on a foundation of public safety. And if you're

55:29.613 --> 55:32.863
looking at the funding of public safety, if that

55:33.363 --> 55:36.976
is your core value, then your core revenue stream,

55:36.992 --> 55:40.591
which is property taxes, really should equate to that.

55:41.105 --> 55:44.389
Now, I know there's a discussion right now, it was just mentioned here a few

55:44.889 --> 55:48.058
minutes ago, maybe we need to make cuts in other places. I, I think what

55:48.558 --> 55:51.989
we're seeing is that there— we don't— Kimberly has said it many times, we don't

55:52.489 --> 55:55.536
have an expense problem, we have a revenue problem. We, we are not keeping up.

55:56.017 --> 55:59.547
And so for years we have run lean. There's really not the area

56:00.047 --> 56:02.612
to cut in other areas to make this happen.

56:03.559 --> 56:06.030
What we need to do is create a policy like,

56:06.672 --> 56:10.106
um, like Councilman, um, Wood has just said.

56:10.780 --> 56:14.150
We need a policy that is perpetuated over time. And if that is to

56:14.650 --> 56:18.292
have public safety safety tied to public property taxes,

56:18.726 --> 56:21.777
we have to recognize that in the state of Utah, we do not

56:22.277 --> 56:25.004
have a cost of living adjustment in our property taxes.

56:25.534 --> 56:28.761
COLA does not affect— you can go

56:28.841 --> 56:32.759
to— I don't know how many of you got a notification

56:33.259 --> 56:37.175
that your Netflix bill went up or that your Disney+ bill went

56:37.675 --> 56:40.627
up or that you pay more at the gas pump or you pay You know,

56:40.643 --> 56:43.999
this hits you all the time, all the way through. True. But people will

56:44.499 --> 56:47.756
complain about local government. They won't necessarily complain about these other areas.

56:48.190 --> 56:51.818
And yet, you know, the cost to the Sheriff's Office Department alone

56:51.867 --> 56:55.255
just for fuel is an issue

56:55.755 --> 56:58.675
with a city of our geographic size. And so if we're gonna do this,

56:58.867 --> 57:02.191
we need to do it with a policy that's in place. And I look at

57:02.223 --> 57:04.824
Saratoga Springs and I say, you know, Saratoga Springs,

57:06.477 --> 57:09.988
They have a very admirable policy that they either— they used

57:10.020 --> 57:13.114
to have, you know, 57 or 58% of

57:13.210 --> 57:16.400
their public safety covered by property taxes, and it has

57:16.496 --> 57:18.917
gone down, and they're just trying to get it back up to that area.

57:19.623 --> 57:22.172
My question when the mayor brought that up to me is, well,

57:23.165 --> 57:26.837
where's the rest of it being paid? Well, it's being— and the

57:27.337 --> 57:29.802
mayor answered me, he said it's being paid from other general fund sources. Well,

57:29.883 --> 57:33.090
what are those other general fund sources? There's sales taxes. There's sales

57:33.590 --> 57:36.910
taxes. Well, who's paying those sales taxes? It's the 70% leakage rate from

57:37.410 --> 57:41.470
Eagle Mountain City into Saratoga, into Lehi, and whatnot. We're paying that difference so

57:41.970 --> 57:45.330
that they're at 100%. And so the more we have Walmart,

57:45.830 --> 57:47.950
the more we have Smith and Lowe's and whatnot, the less that's going to be

57:48.450 --> 57:51.970
impactful. It'll create a greater workload, but it'll mean that we're leaking less

57:52.470 --> 57:56.330
dollars out. But we should never be putting our public safety at

57:56.830 --> 58:00.982
the risk of the fluctuation of sales. It should be a core value

58:01.287 --> 58:04.675
tied to a core resource, and those two

58:04.739 --> 58:08.063
should be married together. And we're not—

58:08.095 --> 58:11.804
we weren't intending to talk about our long-term—

58:12.029 --> 58:15.465
our how to get there. That's probably going to be for the next budget meeting.

58:15.577 --> 58:19.078
Today we're talking about public safety and transportation. We'll do that in

58:19.174 --> 58:22.802
the next budget meeting. But we wanted to make you aware that We

58:23.302 --> 58:26.552
have a fundamental issue that needs to be corrected and policies need to be put

58:27.052 --> 58:29.789
in place. And while it might be admirable for the councils of the past to

58:29.837 --> 58:33.442
look back and say, I didn't raise property taxes, that has really set us

58:33.523 --> 58:36.968
up as a city to be at the very fragile

58:37.468 --> 58:41.230
state that we're at today that you've inherited. So good financial

58:41.730 --> 58:45.172
stability. You look at Provo and other agencies, they have not

58:45.672 --> 58:48.909
only high sales taxes because they're an economic base, And they

58:49.409 --> 58:52.765
have, you know, good fees for services and whatnot, but they also have high property

58:52.782 --> 58:56.590
taxes because they're a fiscally sound, well-managed city with diversified

58:57.090 --> 58:57.283
revenue sources.

59:01.239 --> 59:04.948
While you're thinking, I'm going to add something to that. And this is

59:05.448 --> 59:08.057
just a comment, and then I want to bring it back to kind of how

59:08.122 --> 59:11.684
we tackle this. But last night I went to that debate, same meeting

59:11.717 --> 59:15.478
that Sheriff Smith was at. And every single

59:15.978 --> 59:19.740
person stood up there and said, I won't raise property tax, or boast that I've

59:20.240 --> 59:22.737
8 years, I've never raised property tax. And every time I would holler out,

59:23.106 --> 59:26.567
irresponsible. All you did was kick the can down the road for us to do

59:27.256 --> 59:30.958
it. And so, as much as I don't wanna just raise taxes

59:31.458 --> 59:34.852
or raise taxes, anybody that boasts that they never raised tax just means

59:34.868 --> 59:38.346
that they left the problem for someone else. Now, with that,

59:39.580 --> 59:42.309
But what I want to bring back to the discussion a little bit is then

59:42.405 --> 59:45.520
how do we tackle this with either 7 or 8 or 10.

59:46.676 --> 59:50.160
I wanted, especially for you guys that haven't been here when maybe Councilmember

59:50.208 --> 59:53.773
Wright and I kind of tackled that with McDowell, and I don't

59:54.273 --> 59:56.277
know how I came up with this because I didn't know what I was doing,

59:56.711 --> 59:59.697
but I said, is there a way to do half now and half later?

01:00:00.163 --> 01:00:03.599
And we said, okay, let's give him the 4 I think that he asked

01:00:04.099 --> 01:00:07.449
for in the budget year, which would be a July hire and then 2 more

01:00:07.949 --> 01:00:11.457
in January mid-year because that only costs us half the year's salary and then it's

01:00:11.957 --> 01:00:15.721
ongoing expense. So what I would float out there is some

01:00:16.221 --> 01:00:18.093
kind of— I'm just telling you guys to think about how you want to do

01:00:18.109 --> 01:00:20.578
it. I think that each team needs to have 5 people.

01:00:21.027 --> 01:00:24.634
So I think that immediately as funds come in— don't

01:00:24.682 --> 01:00:28.177
have a heart attack, Kimberly— immediately as we've discussed

01:00:28.209 --> 01:00:31.591
might be September, not July, before we actually

01:00:32.091 --> 01:00:35.441
get this going, and that's a conversation for later, right? But that

01:00:35.941 --> 01:00:39.387
we maybe look at the 4 deputies to add 5 to each team.

01:00:39.451 --> 01:00:42.515
I think that— I understand that Max is overworked, but I know that he's

01:00:42.643 --> 01:00:46.044
tough and he can go maybe till January is what I'm getting at.

01:00:46.493 --> 01:00:49.765
And then you add, and maybe instead of 3 more, we add

01:00:50.022 --> 01:00:53.262
4 more or a 5th one if we get to that. But I'd say 4

01:00:53.310 --> 01:00:57.176
and 4 gets us 8. Do the 4 deputies to

01:00:57.676 --> 01:01:00.883
make sure people are safe because I think that to me That's the first concern.

01:01:00.948 --> 01:01:04.904
And then workload on our proactive stuff might be a mid-year thing.

01:01:06.237 --> 01:01:09.785
So go over here and then back. Okay, so I just want to cover 2

01:01:10.285 --> 01:01:13.072
points. Point number one,

01:01:13.833 --> 01:01:17.137
how we get there, like I said, we're planning on talking about in the next

01:01:17.637 --> 01:01:20.052
meeting. And so we can get into that because we don't want to get into

01:01:20.552 --> 01:01:23.178
the minutiae. We definitely want to— we have a hard stop at 5, so we

01:01:23.195 --> 01:01:27.089
want to make sure you have time to go over your transportation information.

01:01:27.668 --> 01:01:31.171
But the second thing is, the mayor touched on something about September versus July.

01:01:32.023 --> 01:01:35.847
If for— there has been a change to state code this past year, and because

01:01:35.943 --> 01:01:39.398
of that change in state code, whatever you might do or choose to do when

01:01:39.430 --> 01:01:42.998
it comes to a property tax has to go through that

01:01:43.094 --> 01:01:46.340
truth in taxation process that we know you would need to go through.

01:01:47.288 --> 01:01:50.614
But state code changed so that you cannot spend any of those dollars into

01:01:51.114 --> 01:01:55.020
until the truth-in-taxation process is over, which is now 30 days after

01:01:55.149 --> 01:01:58.193
your public hearing in August, which would be September anyway. So we just want to

01:01:58.693 --> 01:02:01.975
make sure it's really clear that if you make any kind of changes, whatever you—

01:02:02.103 --> 01:02:05.805
how you go about, you can decide that later. But if

01:02:06.305 --> 01:02:10.196
you do, it's likely not going to occur until September anyway.

01:02:10.677 --> 01:02:14.458
I just want to bring up a couple, a couple other things maybe good

01:02:14.958 --> 01:02:18.802
for citizens to know, and that is Today, under State

01:02:19.302 --> 01:02:22.922
of Utah law, it's not legal for us to separate out commercial

01:02:23.339 --> 01:02:27.010
property tax and residential. If you have property taxes,

01:02:27.988 --> 01:02:30.793
you can't break it up and say, well, we're just going to have property tax

01:02:31.293 --> 01:02:35.458
for commercial and not for residential. And there's a ratio— what is it, 50 cents

01:02:35.958 --> 01:02:39.274
to a dollar residential to commercial? The only difference is

01:02:39.386 --> 01:02:43.266
your primary home gets a reduction, a 50% reduction, 55% reduction

01:02:43.298 --> 01:02:45.687
on your first home if you live in it and the whole bit.

01:02:46.265 --> 01:02:50.322
Commercial and— it's 45, 45, 55. That's right,

01:02:50.594 --> 01:02:54.603
45% reduction. 45% reduction on your

01:02:55.103 --> 01:02:58.837
primary home. Your secondary home, your rentals and things like that, no. And commercial has

01:02:59.013 --> 01:03:03.006
no reduction. So you get it, you get a TIF on your home of 45%.

01:03:03.279 --> 01:03:06.806
Yes. On your commercial, you pay it at 100%. That's right.

01:03:07.207 --> 01:03:11.413
So, so for those that are thinking Just increase taxes on

01:03:11.445 --> 01:03:15.189
the businesses. They're creating income. My home is not creating income.

01:03:15.237 --> 01:03:18.755
That's a state of Utah law issue. And that's one thing I've brought

01:03:19.255 --> 01:03:23.141
up with John Magnus this next year is to push the legislature to

01:03:23.190 --> 01:03:26.130
have a split tax to give the cities the ability.

01:03:26.483 --> 01:03:30.018
Like we've been talking about data centers, hearing about data centers and all the revenue

01:03:30.403 --> 01:03:34.420
that's going to come pouring out of data centers. And, you know, eventually that

01:03:34.920 --> 01:03:38.127
will be a reality, but that's not a reality reality today. Right now, what we

01:03:38.627 --> 01:03:41.190
have in front of us is there is a need, and we have to find

01:03:41.690 --> 01:03:45.006
a pathway there that may be a property tax increase. But I just want

01:03:45.506 --> 01:03:49.192
to clarify that for the record so citizens know property

01:03:49.692 --> 01:03:53.393
taxes were tied with commercial. So if we increase it for residential,

01:03:53.409 --> 01:03:56.408
it's going to increase commercial and vice versa. It's not a split tax.

01:03:56.889 --> 01:04:00.946
It's probably also important to note that each of

01:04:01.058 --> 01:04:04.337
the data center agreements that we have with RDAs and whatnot have

01:04:04.837 --> 01:04:08.202
it in there contractually that we can't change property tax only on that. Even if

01:04:08.702 --> 01:04:11.632
it wasn't a state of Utah law, we can't change it only on them unless

01:04:11.745 --> 01:04:15.264
we change it citywide anyway. Well said,

01:04:15.360 --> 01:04:19.072
thank you. So I think— I

01:04:19.572 --> 01:04:22.371
don't know that I understand exactly, so I want to clarify. I know what you're

01:04:22.871 --> 01:04:26.136
saying, but so that people understand, let's say we raise

01:04:26.233 --> 01:04:29.385
the tax by $1. What we do is rate tax rate,

01:04:29.401 --> 01:04:33.093
the going to cost the residential unit $1. What you did is raise $1,

01:04:33.125 --> 01:04:36.829
you give 45 cents back to the other person. So the business still

01:04:37.329 --> 01:04:40.674
pays the full $1, but you only pay 55 cents as a homeowner. So if

01:04:41.174 --> 01:04:46.041
you raise it $2, the business pays $2, you only pay $1.10.

01:04:46.957 --> 01:04:50.480
So, so it does go up more for every dollar

01:04:50.980 --> 01:04:54.905
you collect more in the other sites, even though the number's the same.

01:04:55.402 --> 01:04:58.695
You get a rebate for everything you go up. So I want to make sure

01:04:59.195 --> 01:05:02.084
that's clear, that the, the rate that is levied is the same,

01:05:02.630 --> 01:05:06.372
but then the rebate is still the same on the total levy.

01:05:06.645 --> 01:05:10.275
Yeah, I understand that. I think in a perfect world where

01:05:10.339 --> 01:05:14.081
we're looking at commercial and we're excited about that growth,

01:05:14.354 --> 01:05:17.855
in a perfect world we'd say if it's commercial, they're making

01:05:17.872 --> 01:05:21.373
money, they have the means to produce the income to pay a property tax.

01:05:21.727 --> 01:05:24.888
Where my grandma living down the road in retirement,

01:05:25.275 --> 01:05:29.715
her property is not producing any kind of income tax.

01:05:32.060 --> 01:05:34.663
Sorry, I mean that's what the discount on residential already does.

01:05:35.403 --> 01:05:38.472
That's what it already does. It's already set up that way. Well,

01:05:38.729 --> 01:05:42.153
I'm saying for to take residential to zero would

01:05:42.653 --> 01:05:45.711
mean that you'd have to take business to zero, commercial to zero. Yes.

01:05:45.794 --> 01:05:50.393
And a split property tax, you'd have the ability. to say in the future commercial

01:05:50.474 --> 01:05:55.194
pays property taxes, residents don't. Yeah, but even though it's all— it's

01:05:55.307 --> 01:05:58.754
the same rate that's levied, I mean, I know that you all understand it.

01:05:59.044 --> 01:06:02.474
It's 55 cents for a residential unit. It's a dollar for

01:06:02.974 --> 01:06:07.966
every business unit. So I think— I think Councilmember

01:06:08.466 --> 01:06:09.671
Wood has a thought.

01:06:13.443 --> 01:06:16.460
I was at the same meeting that Mayor was at last night, and it was

01:06:16.913 --> 01:06:20.448
disturbing to me how quickly the candidates were like,

01:06:21.235 --> 01:06:24.528
I'm not going to raise taxes and I hate RDAs and they're

01:06:24.608 --> 01:06:27.693
awful and blah blah blah blah blah. And as we were driving home,

01:06:27.757 --> 01:06:31.452
I was ranting to my husband about the fact that

01:06:31.952 --> 01:06:35.147
they don't understand what has happened as a result of it and the taxes we

01:06:35.647 --> 01:06:39.420
collected before versus what we collect now. And I was also

01:06:39.920 --> 01:06:43.099
talking about the residential TIF. So I, I think

01:06:43.180 --> 01:06:46.724
that Even my husband, who hears me talk about this stuff all the time,

01:06:47.254 --> 01:06:51.006
didn't completely understand the 55/45. So we probably

01:06:51.038 --> 01:06:54.694
need to do a better job of helping just everyone understand that.

01:06:55.753 --> 01:06:59.906
Thank you for the presentation that you gave, because over the last

01:06:59.922 --> 01:07:03.241
year, you and I have had many conversations about really

01:07:03.770 --> 01:07:06.320
helping us know what the ideal is,

01:07:07.058 --> 01:07:10.521
a baby step to that, and where we're at right now. And I think that

01:07:11.021 --> 01:07:14.296
you Thank you. You nailed it. I appreciate you breaking down what it costs per

01:07:14.796 --> 01:07:17.990
deputy because there were some really bad misinformation that was

01:07:18.490 --> 01:07:21.589
shared, and I think that this is a better snapshot of that.

01:07:22.231 --> 01:07:26.150
For me, I agree that we should be shooting for that 8 to 10 range

01:07:26.182 --> 01:07:29.700
because it just means that if we don't

01:07:30.200 --> 01:07:32.286
try for that this year, we're going to end up having to do it in

01:07:32.786 --> 01:07:35.515
a subsequent year, and we don't know what the economy is going to be like

01:07:35.804 --> 01:07:39.170
the next time we have to look at this. Um, but for me, I also

01:07:39.186 --> 01:07:43.337
want us to prioritize getting the master offense list updated

01:07:43.837 --> 01:07:47.054
with Eagle Mountain law and citations so that we can be,

01:07:48.064 --> 01:07:52.102
um, enforcing our city code. But I also

01:07:52.262 --> 01:07:55.019
see the need for us, as part of this whole conversation,

01:07:55.451 --> 01:07:59.137
to be looking at the cost of a justice court and moving forward

01:07:59.637 --> 01:08:02.905
on that, because we've been talking about that for over 18 months And we're nowhere

01:08:03.405 --> 01:08:07.323
closer to it. And I do agree that we should get

01:08:07.339 --> 01:08:10.954
serious about creating a policy about

01:08:11.454 --> 01:08:14.633
how public safety funding increases are tied to

01:08:14.714 --> 01:08:18.136
population increases. So I appreciate all the thoughts that have

01:08:18.168 --> 01:08:22.264
been shared, but I do want us to remember master

01:08:22.764 --> 01:08:26.490
offense list and justice court are key components to us addressing

01:08:26.522 --> 01:08:28.803
the concerns that the residents have shared with us.

01:08:32.859 --> 01:08:36.612
Rich, did you got something? If it's— so you're

01:08:37.112 --> 01:08:38.809
good. I just want to make sure that to kind of pull this thing,

01:08:38.825 --> 01:08:42.545
because we do have to get to transportation. Are there any— I know funding is

01:08:42.705 --> 01:08:45.656
kind of what's taking this over, and that's for a future meeting.

01:08:46.185 --> 01:08:49.553
So, uh, specifically on the

01:08:50.053 --> 01:08:53.225
needs and why and call volume, and, you know,

01:08:53.786 --> 01:08:57.718
and you guys were way nicer than I was to Chief, but anything that

01:08:58.218 --> 01:09:01.507
we need to address that way so

01:09:02.007 --> 01:09:03.836
that we can get on to some of the other— So I'll just be quick.

01:09:03.916 --> 01:09:07.288
I mean, basically the reality of it is, is that there is a cost of

01:09:07.448 --> 01:09:11.350
service to have public safety. And as your

01:09:11.850 --> 01:09:15.991
population grows, that cost goes up. There's a, there's a cost per person

01:09:16.440 --> 01:09:19.684
to have a police officer available to come when there's an emergency.

01:09:20.037 --> 01:09:22.684
They're in an accident, accident's happened in their home,

01:09:23.603 --> 01:09:27.117
somebody has perpetrated a crime against them, whatever those things might

01:09:27.617 --> 01:09:31.050
be. And to think that as the population grows that we're covering it with

01:09:31.550 --> 01:09:34.270
that is the same mentality we used to have, that we're going to pay for

01:09:34.302 --> 01:09:37.667
everything by adding more rooftops. That just means our deficit

01:09:38.167 --> 01:09:43.475
gets wider and wider and wider every single year, and then we deficiently produce

01:09:43.975 --> 01:09:47.317
the number of law enforcement officers that are required for that, so that

01:09:47.817 --> 01:09:50.840
the next year it's even worse than it was the year before. That,

01:09:50.953 --> 01:09:54.278
that kind of mentality and policy, we have got to stop doing that.

01:09:54.342 --> 01:09:58.101
And it's not just public safety. We have been operating like that for

01:09:58.601 --> 01:10:02.181
so long. And frankly, if it really is that we have to have an

01:10:02.438 --> 01:10:05.811
unimaginable tax increase to get us back on track,

01:10:06.534 --> 01:10:09.923
then let's be responsible. Other city councils did

01:10:10.423 --> 01:10:13.906
not have the backbone to do it, and they're so proud of not raising taxes

01:10:14.003 --> 01:10:17.565
like that's some big beacon to shine in the sky to say, I screwed

01:10:17.854 --> 01:10:21.207
all the future residents of this city, because that's what's happened.

01:10:22.201 --> 01:10:25.490
And it— I, I take huge offense to listening to people tell us

01:10:25.795 --> 01:10:29.035
how they haven't raised the taxes. All you've done is make it

01:10:29.535 --> 01:10:33.270
so that we can't pay for anything. That's how things are paid for in a

01:10:33.770 --> 01:10:36.335
city, in a county, in the state, the federal government.

01:10:36.784 --> 01:10:41.099
They're paid for by the social contract we all make together as citizens

01:10:41.693 --> 01:10:45.113
For those services to be provided, and that's going to come out of two places:

01:10:45.643 --> 01:10:49.384
our pocketbook when we spend it, and our pocketbook when we where

01:10:49.884 --> 01:10:52.644
we live. And I don't love all the taxes; let's be clear on that.

01:10:53.254 --> 01:10:55.871
But I mean, what what other options do we have?

01:10:57.123 --> 01:11:00.897
Do we just keep pretending like it's not a problem, and go let's

01:11:01.397 --> 01:11:04.381
say we only give him four, and then what next year? He needs twelve,

01:11:04.815 --> 01:11:07.721
or he needs eighteen next year. And so what are we going to do,

01:11:07.785 --> 01:11:11.125
give him 6 till it gets to the point where he's not wrong,

01:11:11.639 --> 01:11:14.994
where we've now created these pocket areas of crime that

01:11:15.494 --> 01:11:18.864
would now need— we need 25, and we start creating these areas where we

01:11:19.364 --> 01:11:22.894
need 30 because police officers don't want to go into this area anymore because

01:11:23.394 --> 01:11:26.234
it becomes so dangerous for them to be in there. It is irresponsible,

01:11:26.330 --> 01:11:29.718
and we need to quit thinking about, oh, let's see how we can fit the

